 ```javascript
const nums = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9];
nums.sort((a, b) => a - b);
console.log(nums);
```

This JavaScript code will sort the given array `nums` in ascending order and log the sorted array to the console, behaving exactly as the original code. The sort function in JavaScript takes a comparison function as an argument, and the comparison function is used to determine the order of the elements. In this case, the comparison function subtracts `b` from `a`, which returns a positive number when `a` should be placed before `b` and a negative number when `b` should be placed before `a`.
